course: dictionary
lesson: 3846
title: Fossilise: rock, time and fixed ideas
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Fossilise is a verb. In science, it means a dead plant or animal is kept in rock over millions of years and slowly turns into a fossil. «Coprolites are fossilised droppings that show what ancient animals ate.» The word fossilised is in the past form, so these droppings have already turned into fossils.
explain: Use the clues around the word. «Amber is fossilised tree resin» tells you the resin is now old and hard, like rock. The word ancient in the same sentence helps too: ancient means very old, so fossilised must link to something that has lasted a very long time.
explain: The word can also describe ideas and habits. «Their ideas had fossilised» means their ideas became fixed and could not change. Test it by swapping in "became fixed": the sentence still makes sense, so this is the second meaning.
fact: Fossilise means to slowly turn into a fossil, preserved in rock over millions of years.
fact: A fossil is the preserved remains or marks of a plant or animal from long ago.
fact: «Fossilised» can also mean fixed and unable to change, as in «Their ideas had fossilised».
fact: Mary Anning found famous fossils on the Jurassic Coast in England.
fact: Sounds do not fossilise, so nobody knows exactly what T. rex sounded like.
exercise: «Coprolites are fossilised droppings that show what ancient animals ate.» What does fossilised mean here? | Fixed and unable to change | Shiny and wet | *Turned into a fossil over a very long time | Very fast and brand new
exercise: «Megalodon's skeleton was cartilage, which rarely fossilises, so mostly teeth remain.» What does rarely mean in this sentence? | Only in summer | *Hardly ever | Very loudly | Every single day
exercise: «Their ideas had fossilised.» Find and copy the word that tells you the ideas became fixed. | *fossilised | had | ideas | Their
test: Jayden says, «Their ideas had fossilised.» What does this mean? | Their ideas were lost in the rock | *Their ideas became fixed and could not change | Their ideas were very loud | Their ideas changed every single week
test: Which swap fits fossilise in the science sense of turning into rock? | Float | Dance | *Be preserved in rock | Melt
exam: Which thing is most likely to fossilise, if the sentence about megalodon is a clue? | Cartilage | *Hard parts like teeth | DNA | Sounds
